---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const MACHINES = [
  { name: 'ZX Spectrum', net: 'Next WiFi / Spectranet', repo: 'rachel-sinclair-zx-spectrum', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Commodore 64', net: 'Offline solo / Ultimate / user-port modem', repo: 'rachel-commodore-64', evidence: 'Solo + reconnect in emulation', hardware: 'Physical evidence pending' },
  { name: 'Commodore VIC-20', net: 'Offline solo / ESP-AT user-port modem', repo: 'rachel-commodore-vic20', evidence: 'v1.0.0: solo + online in emulation', hardware: 'Physical testing pending' },
  { name: 'Commodore Amiga', net: 'Hayes serial modem', repo: 'rachel-amiga', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'BBC Micro', net: 'User-port WiFi bridge', repo: 'rachel-bbc-micro', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Acorn Electron', net: 'WiFi expansion', repo: 'rachel-electron', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Atari 8-bit', net: 'FujiNet', repo: 'rachel-atari8', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Atari 7800', net: 'Cartridge coprocessor', repo: 'rachel-atari-7800', evidence: 'Build only', hardware: 'Design needed' },
  { name: 'Oric Atmos', net: '$031C 6551 + Hayes modem', repo: 'rachel-oric', evidence: 'CI TAP + RUBP v1', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Dragon 32 / CoCo', net: 'Serial / DragonWiFi', repo: 'rachel-dragon', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Model dependent' },
  { name: 'MSX', net: 'GR8NET TCP/IP UNAPI', repo: 'rachel-msx', evidence: 'Emulator protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'ColecoVision', net: '$50–$52 expansion FIFO', repo: 'rachel-coleco-colecovision', evidence: 'Build only', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Sega Master System', net: 'Cartridge adapter', repo: 'rachel-sega-mastersystem', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Sega Game Gear', net: 'Cartridge adapter', repo: 'rachel-sega-gamegear', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Nintendo NES', net: 'Controller-port bridge', repo: 'rachel-nintendo-nes', evidence: 'Emulator protocol', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Nintendo Game Boy', net: 'Buffered link bridge', repo: 'rachel-nintendo-gameboy', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
];

const ORG = 'https://github.com/rachel-multiverse';
const TEST_REPORT = `${ORG}/protocol/issues/new?template=hardware-validation.yml`;
---

<Layout
  title="Ports - Rachel"
  description="Rachel for the VIC-20 and Commodore 64: offline solo play, screenshots and source. Explore the C64 development build with reconnect support."
>
  <main class="ports">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Rachel on vintage computers</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Rachel has a VIC-20 release and a playable C64 development build,
        both with offline solo games. More vintage versions are in development. The shared
        <a href="/protocol">network protocol</a> is designed for multiplayer
        between different machines.
      </p>

      <p class="honest">
        <strong>There is currently no public game server.</strong>
        Vintage online play needs a separately run compatible server.
        The VIC-20 v1.0.0 release is verified in PAL and NTSC emulation, with
        physical hardware testing still pending. Other ports have different
        levels of testing, shown below.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Software evidence and physical-network status for each vintage Rachel client</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Machine</th>
              <th>Networking</th>
              <th>Evidence</th>
              <th>Hardware</th>
              <th>Links</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {MACHINES.map((m) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="machine">{m.name}</td>
                <td class="net">{m.net}</td>
                <td><span class="badge evidence">{m.evidence}</span></td>
                <td><span class="badge hardware">{m.hardware}</span></td>
                <td class="links">
                  <a href={`${ORG}/${m.repo}`}>source</a>
                  <a href={`${ORG}/${m.repo}/actions`}>builds</a>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <section id="vic20">
        <h2>A solo game on the VIC-20</h2>
        <p>
          Load Rachel with an 8KB memory expansion and choose Solo. Pick a table
          size from two to eight seats; computer opponents fill the other seats.
          The game runs locally, so solo play needs neither a modem nor a server.
        </p>
        <p>
          Version 1.0.0 has completed solo and online games in emulation.
          It remains experimental on physical hardware until real-machine testing
          is complete. The release includes the program, controls and testing notes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={`${ORG}/rachel-commodore-vic20/releases`}>VIC-20 releases</a>
          &middot; <a href={`${ORG}/rachel-commodore-vic20`}>Source and setup instructions</a>
        </p>
        <h2>Playing over a network</h2>
        <p>
          In online mode, a server manages the cards and rules. Vintage clients
          show the table and send each player's moves using the shared protocol.
          The VIC-20's online mode has been tested against the Go server in emulation;
          its physical modem connection still needs testing.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no hosted vintage multiplayer service to join at present.
          The iOS app's offline solo and nearby multiplayer modes do not need one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="c64">
        <h2>Pull up a chair at the C64</h2>
        <p>
          A blue screen, a handful of cards and up to seven computer opponents.
          The C64 development build plays a complete solo game on the machine
          itself. Choose Solo, pick a table size from two to eight seats, and
          try to leave someone else holding the cards. No modem or server needed.
        </p>
        <figure class="vintage-shot">
          <img src="/images/c64-solo.png" width="416" height="312" loading="lazy"
            alt="Rachel running in C64 emulation: eight player counts above the discard pile, with the second card highlighted in the player's hand" />
          <figcaption>Eight seats at the table. Captured from the production build in C64 emulation.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h3>Get back to the same game</h3>
        <p>
          Online play connects through an Ultimate interface or a user-port
          WiFi modem. If the connection drops, Rachel tries to recover your
          seat and hand. After three unsuccessful attempts, press R to retry
          or Q to return to the menu.
        </p>
        <p>
          The session is kept while the program stays loaded. Resetting the C64
          starts fresh. Online play needs a separately run Rachel server;
          there is currently no public one to join.
        </p>
        <figure class="vintage-shot">
          <img src="/images/c64-result.png" width="416" height="312" loading="lazy"
            alt="The C64 game-over screen says YOU WENT OUT after a network game that recovered from two forced connection drops" />
          <figcaption>Back at the table: this emulator game finished after two forced disconnects.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h3>What has been tested</h3>
        <p>
          Sixteen automated solo games completed across all seven table sizes.
          Both emulated network transports recovered from two forced drops,
          restored the same seat and hand, and finished the game. Display and
          keyboard checks pass too.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>This is a development build, verified in emulation.</strong>
          Physical C64, Ultimate and modem testing is still pending. The
          2400-baud modem test uses 300 ms between server frames; the setup
          instructions explain that setting.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={`${ORG}/rachel-commodore-64`}>C64 source and build instructions</a>
          &middot; <a href={`${ORG}/rachel-commodore-64/actions`}>Development builds</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What the labels mean</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>CI artifact</strong> means GitHub assembled a downloadable
          test image. <strong>Build + protocol</strong> adds fixture or harness
          checks. <strong>Emulator boot + protocol</strong> also proves the real
          artifact reaches and renders its client screen. <strong>Emulator
          protocol</strong> executes message checks in Emu198x. Hardware labels
          are separate on purpose: a green software build does not prove voltage
          levels, timing, cabling, modem firmware, controls, or a complete game
          on the original machine.
        </p>
        <p class="tester-cta">
          Own one of these machines or adapters?
          <a href={TEST_REPORT}>Run the five-minute smoke test and report it</a>.
          The form asks for reproducible, privacy-safe evidence and credits the
          exact hardware, firmware, client, and server revisions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Writing one yourself</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/protocol">specification</a> is published in full, in
          <a href="https://github.com/rachel-multiverse/protocol">its own
          repository</a> along with the shared conformance fixtures — the
          message vectors a client checks itself against, so a new port can
          prove itself against the format before it ever reaches a real game.
        </p>
        <p>
          Build pages linked above contain the current test artifacts where a
          repository uploads them. They expire with GitHub's artifact retention
          window. For the VIC-20, use the release downloads linked above.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a machine you love is missing, it is missing because nobody has
          started it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p class="back-link"><a href="/">&lsaquo; Back to Rachel</a></p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .ports {
    background: var(--felt-deep);
    padding: 4rem 0;
  }

  .container {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .honest {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
    border-left: 3px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .honest strong { color: var(--text); }

  /* The table is four columns of short values; on a phone it still needs to
     scroll rather than force the page sideways. */
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  caption {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  th, td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  th { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  td { color: var(--text-muted); }

  .machine { color: var(--text); }

  .net { font-size: 0.875rem; }

  .badge {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.18rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .evidence { color: var(--text); background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06); }
  .hardware { color: var(--text-muted); }

  .links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.7rem;
  }

  .links a {
    min-height: 44px;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    touch-action: manipulation;
  }

  .tester-cta {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
  }

  code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
    padding: 0.15em 0.45em;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
  }

  section { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 2rem; }

  .vintage-shot { margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem; }

  .vintage-shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 624px;
    height: auto;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }

  .vintage-shot figcaption {
    max-width: 624px;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  h3 { font-size: 1.125rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem; }

  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  p { color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }
</style>
